 **Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-- **Out of Bounds:** Gaussians near the volume boundary may have contributions falling outside $[0, w), [0, h), [0, c)$. These must be filtered out using the `valid` mask to prevent memory access errors [S2].
-- **Diagonal Covariance Assumption:** The decomposition relies on $C$ being diagonal (or pre-diagonalized) to compute $C^{-1}_{n,d,d}$ efficiently. If $C$ is full, the decomposition $B'^T C^{-1} B'$ cannot be simplified to the sum over $d$ without full matrix multiplication, negating the speedup [S2].
-- **Precision:** The exponential function can underflow for large Mahalanobis distances. Clamping or using log-space arithmetic may be necessary for stability, though not explicitly detailed in the source [S2].
-- **Memory:** Even with decomposition, storing intermediate tensors $T_1, T_2, T_3$ of shape $(n, w_0, h_0, c_0)$ requires significant VRAM if $n$ is large. The source notes 16.87 GiB for $n=150,000$ and local size 17 [S2].
